The lightest approach is an **ambient `TimeProvider`** (built into .NET 8+):

1. Replace `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow` calls with `TimeProvider.System.GetUtcNow()` (or accept a `TimeProvider` parameter defaulting to `TimeProvider.System`).

2. In tests, pass `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
// Production code
public class Invoice
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _clock;
    public Invoice(TimeProvider? clock = null) => _clock = clock ?? TimeProvider.System;
    public DateTimeOffset CreatedAt => _clock.GetUtcNow();
}

// Test
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var invoice = new Invoice(fake);
Assert.Equal(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero), invoice.CreatedAt);

fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(30)); // move time forward
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**

- `TimeProvider` is in `System` (no extra package for production code).
- `FakeTimeProvider` comes from the small `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet package (test project only).
- No interfaces to define, no DI container needed—just an optional constructor parameter.

If you're on **.NET 7 or earlier**, define your own minimal abstraction:

```csharp
public abstract class Clock
{
    public static Clock System { get; } = new SystemClock();
    public abstract DateTimeOffset UtcNow { get; }
    private sealed class SystemClock : Clock
    {
        public override DateTimeOffset UtcNow => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
    }
}
```

Then swap in a test double the same way.
